    "https://pacific.peopleadmin.com/postings/32955": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Department of Speech Language-Pathology (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "University of the Pacific Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor, Department of Speech Language-Pathology (Tenure-Track) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate Professor, Department of Speech Language-Pathology (Tenure-Track) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. For Applicants Seeking Job Opportunities within the University Internal: Internal applicants will be considered within the first five (5) business days of the posting period. External: External applicants will be considered on the sixth (6) business day of the posting period. Posting Details Position Information Title Assistant/Associate Professor, Department of Speech Language-Pathology (Tenure-Track) Campus Stockton Department Speech Language Pathology Posting Number F01112 Full or Part Time Full Time Open Date 10/20/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Days Per Week Weeks Per Year Position Description Primary Purpose and Essential Functions The department of Speech-Language Pathology at the University of the Pacific invites applications for a 9-month tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor. Area of expertise is open, with preference given to candidates with expertise in adult neurogenic communication disorders. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching, advising and mentoring, research and scholarship, professional and university service . The Department enrolls approximately 140 majors: 60 Master’s degree students and 80 undergraduates. Faculty consists of six tenured or tenure-track speech-language pathologists. Two full-time Clinical Directors as well as two Clinical Coordinators are also integral members of the team. University of the Pacific recognizes that diversity, equity, and inclusion is foundational to the success of our valued students and employees. We prioritize policy and decision-making that demonstrates awareness of, and responsiveness to, the ways socio-cultural forces related to race, gender, ability, sexuality, socio-economic status, etc. impede or propel students, faculty, and staff. Pacific offers full-time employees a unique opportunity to live on our beautiful campus at below-market rental rates—helping you save time and money on your commute. Minimum Qualifications Terminal degrees (e.g. PhD, Ed.D, SLPD , DHSc) in Speech-Language Pathology, Speech and Hearing Science or a closely related field; doctoral candidates who are ABD will also be considered. ASHA Certificate of Clinical Competence in Speech-Language Pathology. Possess or be eligible for a California Speech-Language Pathology license. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in Speech-Language Pathology, Speech and Hearing Science or a closely related field. Evidence of potential for excellence in teaching and research activities. Experience and sensitivity in working with people of diverse backgrounds and cultures. Demonstrated experience in advancing social justice, equity, and inclusion in a university setting. Ability to engage and integrate culturally responsive practices and knowledge in their work. Physical Requirements The physical demands described here are representative but not definitive of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Work Environment/Work Week/Travel: Work is performed during standard hours; Additional hours may be required to meet business needs or deadlines Hiring Range $110,000 - $115,000 per year. We consider factors such as, but not limited to, scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s qualifications, internal equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Special Instructions This recruitment will remain open until filled. Contact Information For additional information, contact Valerie McDaniel, PhD, CCC - SLP , MT-BC., Search Committee Chair Department of Speech-Language Pathology at vmcdaniel@pacific.edu . Dr. McDaniel will be available at the ASHA Conference in Indianapolis if interested applicants would like to discuss the open position. Please schedule with her directly via her Pacific email vmcdaniel@pacific.edu Contact Email vmcdaniel@pacific.edu Reference Letter Information Are Applicants Required to Submit References for This Posting? Yes Minimum Number of References 3 Maximum Number of References 3 Background Check Statement All applicants who receive a conditional offer of employment are required to execute a release and authorization for a background screening. AB 810 Misconduct Disclosure Requirement: University of the Pacific complies with California Assembly Bill 810, requiring candidates accepting conditional job offers to disclose any final administrative or judicial findings, ongoing proceedings, allegations, resignations under investigation, or appeals related to sexual harassment or misconduct within the past seven years. Anti-Discrimination/EEO Policy Statement University of the Pacific is an equal opportunity employer dedicated to workforce diversity across backgrounds, experiences, and viewpoints. Pacific does not unlawfully discriminate in its hiring of faculty and staff, or in the provision of its employment benefits to its faculty and staff on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, ancestry, age, genetic information, sex/gender, marital status, military and veteran status, sexual orientation, medical condition, pregnancy, gender identity, gender expression, or mental or physical disability, or other legally protected characteristics or combination of such characteristics. While we strive to attract a broad and representative pool of candidates, all hiring decisions are made based on merit, selecting the most qualified individual for each position. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a terminal degree (e.g. PhD, Ed.D, SLPD, DHSc) in Speech-Language Pathology, Speech and Hearing Science or a closely related field; or are you an ABD doctoral candidate? Yes No * Do you possess or are you eligible for a California Speech-Language Pathology license? Yes No * Do you have an ASHA Certificate of Clinical Competence in Speech-Language Pathology? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Other Document Other Document 2 Other Document 3 University of the Pacific / 3601 Pacific Ave, Stockton, CA 95211 human_resources@pacific.edu human_resources@pacific.edu / 209.946.2124 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://pacific.peopleadmin.com/postings/32962": {
      "title": "Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor",
      "description": "University of the Pacific Applicant Portal | Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Job Title Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor Union Level Department Counseling and Psychological Services Campus Stockton Posting Number 201304395P Full or Part Time Full Time Number of Months 12 Work Schedule Position End Date Open Date 10/21/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions to Applicants For Applicants Seeking Job Opportunities within the University Internal: Internal applicants will be considered within the first five (5) business days of the posting period. External: External applicants will be considered on the sixth (6) business day of the posting period. Sponsorship This position is not eligible for a visa sponsorship now or in the future. Position Summary Information Primary Purpose The Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor ( LPCC ) provides culturally responsive mental health services to students in a higher education setting. Responsibilities include clinical assessment, diagnosis, individual and group counseling, crisis intervention, consultation, outreach programming, case management, and collaboration with campus partners. The LPCC promotes student well-being, persistence, and academic success through evidence-based clinical care and prevention-focused initiatives. Pacific offers full-time employees a unique opportunity to live on our beautiful campus at below-market rental rates—helping you save time and money on your commute. Essential Functions Provide brief individual, couples, and group counseling to diverse college and graduate student populations. Conduct comprehensive clinical assessments, biopsychosocial evaluations, risk assessments, and diagnostic evaluations. Develop and implement treatment plans utilizing evidence-based therapeutic interventions. Maintain timely and accurate clinical documentation in accordance with professional, legal, ethical, and institutional standards. Provide referrals to community providers, psychiatric services, higher levels of care, and campus resources as appropriate. Deliver services through in-person and telehealth modalities. Provides preventative outreach/programming and trainings, and represents CAPS at campus events such as orientation, trainings, critical incident debriefing, and workshops on an as needed basis. Consults with faculty and staff within the University regarding student concerns and needs. Participates in university-wide committees. Participates in staff, multidisciplinary team, and Student Life Divisional meetings. Attends professional development opportunities and maintains licensure status with the appropriate California board. Performs other duties as assigned. University of the Pacific recognizes that diversity, equity, and inclusion is foundational to the success of our valued students and employees. We prioritize policy and decision-making that demonstrates awareness of, and responsiveness to, the ways socio-cultural forces related to race, gender, ability, sexuality, socio-economic status, etc. impede or propel students, faculty, and staff. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in Licensed Professional Clinical Counseling, Counseling, or closely related field from an accredited institution. Current California Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor ( LPCC ) license in good standing. Minimum of two (2) years prior experience with adolescents or adults. Experience providing crisis intervention. Knowledge of college student development and mental health concerns affecting young adults. Demonstrated commitment to multicultural counseling and inclusive practices. Ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds and identities. Preferred Qualifications Experience working in a college counseling center of university setting. Three (3) or more years of post-licensure clinical experience Experience with outreach programs Experience with Point N Click electronic medical records. Ability to teach in an informal workshop setting. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Strong interpersonal skills as demonstrated through the ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with other departments within the university. Bilingual/Multilingual. Experience and sensitivity in working with people of diverse backgrounds and cultures. Demonstrated experience in advancing social justice, equity, and inclusion in a university setting. Physical Requirements The physical demands described here are representative but not definitive of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. This is an in-person role, and the successful candidate must be willing to relocate within California, preferably within 25 miles of the Stockton Pacific campus. Frequent sitting and verbal communication with occasional walking and standing. Infrequent bending, stooping, kneeling, or reaching above shoulders. May be expected to lift up to 25 lbs. Use of computers, phones, and other technology. Hiring Range $80,000 - $90,000 per year. We consider factors such as, but not limited to, scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's qualifications, internal equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Background Check Statement All applicants who receive a conditional offer of employment are required to execute a release and authorization for a background screening. AB 810 Misconduct Disclosure Requirement: University of the Pacific complies with California Assembly Bill 810, requiring candidates accepting conditional job offers to disclose any final administrative or judicial findings, ongoing proceedings, allegations, resignations under investigation, or appeals related to sexual harassment or misconduct within the past seven years. Anti-Discrimination/EEO Policy Statement University of the Pacific is an equal opportunity employer dedicated to workforce diversity across backgrounds, experiences, and viewpoints. Pacific does not unlawfully discriminate in its hiring of faculty and staff, or in the provision of its employment benefits to its faculty and staff on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, ancestry, age, genetic information, sex/gender, marital status, military and veteran status, sexual orientation, medical condition, pregnancy, gender identity, gender expression, or mental or physical disability, or other legally protected characteristics or combination of such characteristics. While we strive to attract a broad and representative pool of candidates, all hiring decisions are made based on merit, selecting the most qualified individual for each position. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a Master’s degree in Licensed Professional Clinical Counseling, Counseling, or a closely related field from an accredited institution? Yes No * Do you possess a current California Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor (LPCC) license in good standing? Yes No * Do you have a minimum of two (2) years of prior experience working with adolescents or adults? Yes No * Do you have experience providing crisis intervention, and have knowledge of college student development and mental health concerns affecting young adults? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Other Document Other Document 2 University of the Pacific / 3601 Pacific Ave, Stockton, CA 95211 human_resources@pacific.edu human_resources@pacific.edu / 209.946.2124 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
